 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server pushes each revision as it commits, giving subsecond updates without a realtime vendor or its recurring fees. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were considered and rejected (see Key Design Decisions).
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+This choice shapes the three unbuilt components. The change store keeps a replayable revision history so a socket can resume where a client left off. Delivery owns socket sessions — authentication, connection capacity, and reliable publication to connected clients. Browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up: on reconnect, the browser resumes from its last seen revision rather than refetching the whole board.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 - Access: `docs/intent/access/access-design.md`, with `docs/intent/access/access-specs.md`. Written below the stable HTTPS command boundary.
-- Change store: `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`, with `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-specs.md`. Not written yet. Revision history, read indexes, and any publication outbox depend on the delivery approach.
-- Delivery: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`, with `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns polling responses, socket sessions, or hosted publication, depending on the approach.
-- Browser sync: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`, with `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-specs.md`. Not written yet. Its subscription, reconnect, and catch-up behavior depend on the approach.
+- Change store: `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`, with `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-specs.md`. Not written yet. Must keep a replayable revision history that lets a reconnecting browser resume from its last seen revision.
+- Delivery: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`, with `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns self-hosted WebSocket sessions: connection authentication, capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, and reliable publication of each committed revision to connected clients.
+- Browser sync: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`, with `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns the WebSocket subscription, reconnect, and catch-up from the last seen revision after a disconnect of up to 20 minutes.
 
 ## Tenets
 
+- Self-hosted infrastructure over realtime vendors. The board's realtime channel runs on our own servers so connection behavior stays in our hands and no recurring vendor fee or provider coupling sits on the critical path.
 - A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route.
 - Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.
 - Commands use server-side authorization; browser state is not evidence of permission.
